From 1761f9be6167e2fa7b82268380912849adb34e64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:19:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: pair the pictures of a two-track stream by the
 times of its tracks

In a Dolby Vision stream of profile 7 the base and the enhancement
layer can be coded in different picture orders: the enhancement access
unit that travels with a base picture is then the one of another
picture. In the test stream that is so for 2822 of 2876 access units.
The enhancement-layer module therefore pairs the decoded pictures by
picture order count, counted in each layer from a random-access picture
that both layers have with the same count. After a seek, or when the
layer has to start again, the base pictures go out without the layer
until such a picture comes.

A stream that comes in two tracks (the transport stream of a UHD
Blu-ray) says more: each access unit of the enhancement track has its
own presentation time, and the picture it belongs to is the base
picture of that time. The join of the two tracks threw that away. It
now passes it on with each joined packet (strings metadata, key
"dvbridge_layer_time") as the difference to the presentation time of
the packet, which stays right when the player moves the times of the
packets. The module pairs by it:

- the enhancement decoder gets each access unit with that time, and a
  base picture takes the enhancement picture of its time, to the half
  millisecond that the conversions may cost (pictures are at least
  four milliseconds apart);
- the layer starts at any of its random-access pictures, since the time
  says which base picture that one belongs to. In the test stream,
  after seeks to five places, the first picture with its layer was
  picture 17, 3, 19, 4 and 10 of those shown, where by picture order it
  was 53, 18, 46, 19 and 59;
- nothing is kept, or asked of the base layer, by picture order. A base
  packet without a time (the last one of the test stream) made the
  whole layer start again; now it only leaves its own access unit
  without one, and the last five pictures with a layer keep it.

A stream of one track brings no such time and is paired as before.

A time is only as good as what happens to it on its way. Four things
do, and each could pair a base picture with the enhancement picture of
a neighbour (independent reviews found them; each was reproduced in
the host test before it was closed):

- a base packet without its presentation time. The decoder makes up a
  time for its picture, which is the time of another picture as likely
  as not: with packet 61 of the test stream without its time, its
  picture got the enhancement picture that packet 58 carried. A picture
  whose packet had no time is not paired (VideoPicture::doviSourceTimed);
- the start of the file. Kodi's demuxer takes the start time off every
  time after it, makes 0 of a time up to half a second before it, and
  leaves an earlier one as it is, so the difference between two times
  is not always the difference between the two times it makes. With
  the start one to eleven pictures into the test stream and the base
  layer decoded by one thread, five pictures in eight runs get another
  one's enhancement picture. The demuxer now checks each difference
  against its own times (CDVLayerMerge::CheckLayerTime()): where the
  two were moved apart it puts the difference between its two times,
  and where the access unit is presented in that half second, whose
  times are all 0, it marks the time as not usable. Once for each
  packet: the demuxer keeps the first packet after a seek and reads it
  again, and a second look took a difference that the first had put in
  microseconds for one in the ticks of the packet (found by a review;
  the check also leaves any entry that is not in the time base of the
  packet). And the time 0, once two base packets had it, pairs no
  picture. The module is told which time stands for that 0
  (dvbridge_fel_time_zero()): a player that has taken something off
  the times before (it keeps what it took for a jump in what it played
  earlier) takes it off that 0 too, and the packets of that half second
  then come with one other time. Taken for a time that came again,
  that started the layer again (in 6 of 16 runs with the start one to
  eleven pictures into the test stream and 7.25 s, or an hour the
  other way, taken off the times). That base packets had that time is
  not forgotten when the layer starts again for a failure of its own:
  their pictures are still to come out of the base decoder. With the
  start of the file on a random-access unit of the layer and the layer
  started again just before it, the first picture of the time 0 got
  that unit (found by a review, and reproduced). Only a flush of the
  base decoder forgets it (dvbridge_fel_base_flushed()): no picture
  from before it is asked for afterwards;
- the player joining a jump in the stream to its clock: every time
  from a packet on is moved, between an access unit and its base
  picture. Each packet now says by how much its times were moved in
  all (DemuxPacket::m_ptsOffset; m_ptsOffsetCorrection is the last
  amount only). When that changes the layer starts again, and the base
  pictures of packets from before, which the decoder still gives for a
  while, are not paired: the decoder hands the count of the changes on
  to the picture of each packet (AV_CODEC_FLAG_COPY_OPAQUE, which Kodi
  sets and did not use). A flush counts as a change too;
- times that go back without a word from the player (a stream that was
  spliced, with a jump the player does not see): the times that come
  again belong to other pictures, and the layer still holds pictures
  of the times before. Without the player's count, two pictures in
  four such runs got another one's enhancement picture. The module now
  keeps the times of its last 64 base packets, from the first one on:
  one that comes again starts the layer again, unless it is the time
  that stands for 0 (above).

An access unit without a usable time in a stream that gives them
cannot be paired, so it is not decoded: a hole in the layer, which
costs the pictures that need it (FFmpeg's decoder refuses a picture
that refers to one it does not have). By times the decoder gives its
pictures in the order they are presented, so an enhancement picture of
a later time that waits says that the one asked for was not decoded:
its base picture goes out without the layer at once, and the layer
goes on (those pictures are counted, and the log says how many when
the stream ends). Late pictures start it again only after 96 in a
row, where by picture order it is three: starting again threw away the
next random-access picture of the layer when it had just come. In the
test stream a hole costs one picture, 21 at a reference picture and 24
at a random-access picture of the layer (its group), and never a
restart.

When the stream says nothing of the time of an access unit whose
packet has its own, more than eight times without 240 timed ones in a
row between them, it times only some of its access units, which
picture order pairs and times do not: it is paired by picture order
from then on. Packets without any time (the player marks them so while
it is not sure of a jump) and times that could not be passed on do not
count towards that: picture order would not pair those either. Nine
such packets in a row made the module go over to picture order for
good, and then start the layer again with each further one; now they
cost 15 pictures, and 21 of them 54.

While the layer waits for a picture to start at, an access unit that
its parser cannot read no longer fails the layer, which was then
started again with every such unit: it cannot start it, like any other.
After a seek the parser may have the parameter sets of the layer only
from its next random-access picture on, which is where a stream of two
tracks brings them. (The test stream did not show it: the parser of the
layer is set up with the parameter sets of the base layer, and its
access units could be read with those.)

The text of a time is read strictly, and alike, by the module and by
the check of the demuxer (decimal numbers, one space, no more, and not
the least number, which stands for no time); two enhancement
pictures of one time fail the layer, to the same half millisecond; a
base packet without a time, by picture order, gives its reason to the
log; and 240 base pictures in a row without their enhancement picture
are said once.

Host tests with the two-track test stream, decoded in software and
paired as CDVDVideoCodecFFmpeg does it, with its limits for pictures
that wait (tests/fel_tracks_test.c, tests/dvmerge_test.cpp). Which
enhancement picture a base picture got is known by its access unit,
not by its samples (348 of the 2875 enhancement pictures of the stream
look like another one, and two of the wrong pairs above were of
pictures that look alike): every packet is numbered, both decoders hand
the number on to their pictures, and a pair is right when the stream
presents the one access unit at the time of the other picture.

- every joined packet says the time that the enhancement track itself
  has for that access unit; each of those times is the presentation
  time of one base picture, and no two are the same;
- pairing by the times and pairing by picture order, which know nothing
  of each other, give each of the 2870 pictures that both pair the
  enhancement picture of its time;
- by the times, the pictures paired are exactly those whose enhancement
  access unit the stream gave from the first random-access picture of
  the layer on, in the whole stream and after each seek, none late,
  and the layer never started again; the same with the base layer
  decoded by one thread, which gives its pictures as early as a
  hardware decoder does;
- the same when the module has no parameter sets but those in the
  stream. With the change for unreadable access units taken out, that
  run fails: the layer is started again 18 times, once for each access
  unit before its first random-access picture;
- 71 runs with times that are missing or were moved (holes around a
  random-access picture of the layer, one access unit in 250 without
  its time, a base packet without its presentation time at twelve
  places, 9 and 21 packets without any time, eight start times, ten
  moves of the times): no picture gets the enhancement picture of
  another time. Thirty of those runs leave one protection out ("as it
  was"). What they show depends on when the base decoder gives its
  pictures: with eight threads, only the first protection; with one
  thread, which gives them as early as a hardware decoder does, also
  the check of the start time (five pictures; the test fails when that
  number is 0) and the times that go back (two pictures with the
  module before this rule, none and four restarts with it). No run
  shows a wrong pair where the player makes the times later, with or
  without its count: that protection stays as the direct answer;
- the eight start times again with something taken off every time from
  the first packet on (16 runs): the same pictures are paired as
  without that, and the layer does not start again;
- the start of the file on each of three random-access units of the
  layer, the layer started again after each of the packets around the
  unit and after the first one of the time 0, also with something
  taken off the times (25 runs): no picture of the time 0 is paired.
  Played from a random-access picture of the base layer with the times
  given only from the unit on (2 runs), the same. A file that starts
  with its first picture, played again after a flush: that picture,
  the only one of the time 0, is paired both times. Times moved back
  by twelve pictures before the stream gives its times: no restart
  for a time that came twice. Each of these fails with the change it
  is there for taken out of the module (four changes, one at a time);
  what the module keeps when a single base packet had the time 0
  before a restart is not exercised by this stream;
- the start times, with and without something taken off the times,
  and the moves again with the two tracks in each other's place (60
  runs, and the whole stream both ways). The base
  track of the test stream is coded in the order it is presented and
  its enhancement track is not, so that no run had a base decoder that
  reorders its pictures; swapped, the base layer is the reordered one:
  2867 pictures paired by both ways, none wrongly, none late;
- CheckLayerTime() with Kodi's conversion: differences after the
  start, at it, across it, within the half second before it and before
  that, and each kind looked at twice;
- with every second access unit without its time, the module goes over
  to picture order after nine, and stays there when the stream is
  played again with all its times;
- no memory is leaked (LeakSanitizer).

The eight start times of those runs put the first random-access
picture of the stream before the start of the file, which no file
does (its start time is at most the time of its first packet): there
the first group of pictures goes out without the layer. In a file
that was cut and begins with pictures that lead its first
random-access picture, those are what is presented before the start;
their enhancement pictures are not decoded, and the first picture of
the film goes out without its layer when such a base packet follows
it (its time, 0, is then the time of two packets).

Not run in Kodi nor on hardware; the parts of the demuxer and the
decoder around the module are written again in the test, not built.
